### Area 1: Spark's server side (P-95, step 1). A question is answered through the AI gateway
### with three read-only tools that run as the person asking: check_stock, find_orders and
### get_order. With AI_PROVIDER unset the offline mock answers, from the tool's own result.
### Sign in first (requests/auth.http), then paste the op_csrf cookie value below.
### Every route needs spark.use: every role but the System admin holds it by default.
### Conversations are the caller's own: another person's id answers 404 like an unknown one,
### and in a demo session they are kept per sign-in session.
@baseUrl = http://localhost:3000
@csrf = paste the op_csrf cookie value here after signing in
@conversationId = paste a conversation id from the create response here

### Start a conversation. The title is optional: without one the first question names it.
POST {{baseUrl}}/api/v1/spark/conversations
Content-Type: application/json
X-CSRF-Token: {{csrf}}

{}

### The caller's own conversations, newest first. Paged by page and pageSize.
GET {{baseUrl}}/api/v1/spark/conversations?page=1&pageSize=25

### One conversation with its messages.
GET {{baseUrl}}/api/v1/spark/conversations/{{conversationId}}

### Ask a question (1 to 2000 characters). The answer comes back stored and audited, with the
### steps (each tool the model asked for, and whether it ran, was refused or failed) and the
### sources (the items and orders the tools returned: what Spark looked at, not proof of the
### prose). A price or an amount your role may not see is hidden from the model too.
### A failed model call is still 201: read data.answer.status (OK or FAILED) and
### data.answer.errorCode. A tool the person may not use is FAILED with errorCode refused.
### 409: a question in this conversation is still waiting for its answer.
### 429 RATE_LIMITED: too many questions in a minute (SPARK_QUESTIONS_PER_MINUTE) or in a day
### (SPARK_QUESTIONS_PER_DAY).
POST {{baseUrl}}/api/v1/spark/conversations/{{conversationId}}/messages
Content-Type: application/json
X-CSRF-Token: {{csrf}}

{ "question": "How many HP monitors do we have?" }

### More questions the mock answers with a tool: an order, an item, a customer's orders,
### the orders short of stock. Anything else gets what Spark can do and no step.
POST {{baseUrl}}/api/v1/spark/conversations/{{conversationId}}/messages
Content-Type: application/json
X-CSRF-Token: {{csrf}}

{ "question": "What is the state of SO-0001?" }

### Refused (400): the question takes one field and nothing else.
POST {{baseUrl}}/api/v1/spark/conversations/{{conversationId}}/messages
Content-Type: application/json
X-CSRF-Token: {{csrf}}

{ "question": "Hello", "model": "another-model" }

### Archive a conversation. It is kept, no longer listed, and reads as 404 afterwards.
DELETE {{baseUrl}}/api/v1/spark/conversations/{{conversationId}}
X-CSRF-Token: {{csrf}}

### Refused (403): signed in as the System admin. The refusal is audited as auth.permission.refused.
